pjsua: answer the connected-party display INFO instead of 500-ing it

A transferred call keeps its dialog. 4023 calls 4022, 4022 transfers the call to 4021, and
nothing in the SIP that reaches the remaining party says the person on the other end has
changed -- same Call-ID, same From, same To, same tags. FreeSWITCH closes that gap with an
in-dialog INFO carrying the new party in two private headers:

    INFO sip:6001@192.168.30.202:5088;ob SIP/2.0
    From: "tccs4" <sip:4022@192.168.20.56>;tag=H296pKUZ8mtjS
    Content-Type: message/update_display
    Content-Length: 0
    X-FS-Display-Name: 4021
    X-FS-Display-Number: 4021
    X-FS-Lazy-Attended-Transfer: true

It is only ever sent to an endpoint that first advertised `X-FS-Support: update_display`,
so an endpoint that receives one asked for it.

pjsua answers an incoming INFO in exactly three cases, all of them keyed off the message
BODY: application/media_control+xml, application/dtmf-relay and
application/trickle-ice-sdpfrag. This INFO has no body at all. It therefore matched none
of them, no dialog usage claimed it either -- mod_inv_on_rx_request() reports only INVITE,
CANCEL, BYE and ACK -- and pjsip_dlg_on_rx_request() fell through to its last resort:

    500 Internal Server Error / "Unhandled by dialog usages"

on a request the endpoint had asked to be sent.

Reading the update was never the problem: the whole message reaches the application
through on_call_tsx_state(). RESPONDING is, and from pjsua2 it is impossible. `Call`
exposes sendRequest(), which puts a new request into the dialog, and has no API of any
kind for responding to a request that arrived -- SipRxData carries the bytes and a void*
and nothing that can answer them. The transaction only exists down here, so this is where
the response has to be built.

So one more branch on the same chain, matched on the Content-Type header rather than on a
body that does not exist. rdata->msg_info.ctype is filled by the parser whenever the
header is present (sip_parser.c, parse_hdr_content_type) and is NULL on an INFO that
carried none, so `body == NULL && ctype is message/update_display` names this message and
no other. Every other INFO keeps the behaviour it had, 500 included: this claims one
content type, answers it, and interprets nothing -- what the headers mean stays the
application's business.

Upstream would need a general "unhandled INFO" callback to fix this properly, which is a
larger change to the pjsua callback surface than this repository should carry.

diff --git a/third_party/pjproject/pjsip/src/pjsua-lib/pjsua_call.c b/third_party/pjproject/pjsip/src/pjsua-lib/pjsua_call.c
index 9b8e16db..b3736d1a 100644
--- a/third_party/pjproject/pjsip/src/pjsua-lib/pjsua_call.c
+++ b/third_party/pjproject/pjsip/src/pjsua-lib/pjsua_call.c
@@ -6439,6 +6439,8 @@ static void pjsua_call_on_tsx_state_changed(pjsip_inv_session *inv,
     const pj_str_t STR_MEDIA_CONTROL_XML = { "media_control+xml", 17 };
     const pj_str_t STR_DTMF_RELAY        = { "dtmf-relay", 10 };
     const pj_str_t STR_TRICKLE_ICE_SDP   = { "trickle-ice-sdpfrag", 19 };
+    const pj_str_t STR_MESSAGE           = { "message", 7 };
+    const pj_str_t STR_UPDATE_DISPLAY    = { "update_display", 14 };
 
     pjsua_call *call;
 
@@ -6817,6 +6819,48 @@ static void pjsua_call_on_tsx_state_changed(pjsip_inv_session *inv,
                 status = pjsip_tsx_send_msg(tsx, tdata);
         }
 
+        /* Check connected-party display update in the INFO message.
+         *
+         * FreeSWITCH announces a transfer that happened elsewhere with an in-dialog
+         * INFO of type message/update_display, carrying the new party in
+         * X-FS-Display-Name and X-FS-Display-Number. It has NO BODY -- Content-Length
+         * is 0 -- so the three tests above cannot match it, and nothing else in pjsip
+         * or pjsua claims a bodyless INFO either. The request therefore reached the end
+         * of pjsip_dlg_on_rx_request() unprocessed and was answered "500 Unhandled by
+         * dialog usages" (sip_dialog.c), which is a protocol error on a request this
+         * endpoint asked to be sent: FreeSWITCH only sends it to an endpoint that
+         * advertised X-FS-Support: update_display.
+         *
+         * The content is read by the application, which sees the whole message through
+         * on_call_tsx_state(). What the application cannot do is RESPOND: pjsua2 exposes
+         * Call::sendRequest for putting a request into the dialog and nothing at all for
+         * responding to one that arrived. So the 200 is sent from here, which is the
+         * only place that still holds the transaction, and the body of the update stays
+         * the application's to interpret.
+         *
+         * Matched on the Content-Type header rather than on the message body, because
+         * there is no body: rdata->msg_info.ctype is filled by the parser whenever the
+         * header is present (sip_parser.c, parse_hdr_content_type) and is NULL on an
+         * INFO that carried none. An INFO of any other type is left exactly as it was,
+         * 500 included -- this claims one content type and nothing else.
+         */
+        else if (body == NULL && rdata->msg_info.ctype &&
+                 pj_stricmp(&rdata->msg_info.ctype->media.type, &STR_MESSAGE)==0 &&
+                 pj_stricmp(&rdata->msg_info.ctype->media.subtype,
+                            &STR_UPDATE_DISPLAY)==0)
+        {
+            pjsip_tx_data *tdata;
+            pj_status_t status;
+
+            PJ_LOG(4,(THIS_FILE, "Call %d: connected party updated by INFO",
+                      call->index));
+
+            status = pjsip_endpt_create_response(tsx->endpt, rdata, 200, NULL,
+                                                 &tdata);
+            if (status == PJ_SUCCESS)
+                status = pjsip_tsx_send_msg(tsx, tdata);
+        }
+
     } else if (tsx->role == PJSIP_ROLE_UAC && 
                pjsip_method_cmp(&tsx->method, &pjsip_info_method)==0 &&
